# Instance environment variables - TEMPLATE WITH PLACEHOLDERS ONLY.
#
# These are set on the Unikraft Cloud *instance* (console: instance ->
# Environment, or the unikraft CLI's -e flags), never baked into the image
# and never committed with real values. The boot hook reads them at
# runtime; one image can serve any number of instances this way.
#
# CLI notes (both learned the hard way):
#   * Env edits are silently IGNORED while the instance is running -
#     stop the instance first, edit, then start it.
#   * -e replaces the WHOLE env table. Pass every key every time, or the
#     ones you leave out are deleted.

# --- Base zvps services (consumed by the image's own entrypoint) ---

# SSH login user for the guest.
SSH_USER=zv
# SSH login password. Use a strong unique one; anyone with it owns the box.
SSH_PWD=<choose-a-strong-password>
# Password for the ttyd web terminal (published on the instance endpoint).
TTYD_P1=<choose-a-strong-password>

# --- argosbx autostart (consumed by /home/zv/argosbx-run.sh) ---

# Local port for the VMess-WS inbound that the tunnel forwards to.
VMPT=8001
# VMess UUID. Generate your own, e.g. `cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid`
# or PowerShell: [guid]::NewGuid(). Clients need this exact value.
UUID=<your-vmess-uuid>
# Public hostname of your Cloudflare Tunnel, e.g. tunnel.example.com.
# In Cloudflare DNS it is a proxied CNAME to <tunnel-id>.cfargotunnel.com.
AGN=<your-tunnel-hostname>
# Cloudflare Tunnel token (the long eyJ... string). Get it from the
# Cloudflare dashboard: Zero Trust -> Networks -> Tunnels -> your tunnel
# -> "Install and run a connector" - the token in the run command.
# Treat it like a password: it lets anyone run your tunnel.
AGK=<your-cloudflare-tunnel-token>
